[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
Not an mswitch0 fault. The failure is on router16’s routing subsystem (FRRouting/OSPF) on the uplink subnet to mswitch0.

Affected component: router16’s FRR/OSPF daemon on the 10.2.2.0/24 transit segment (interface bond0, IP 10.2.2.1). On mswitch0, OSPF on br0 (10.2.2.2) has no neighbor.

Mechanism: With ospfd down on router16, mswitch0 loses its OSPF adjacency and thus withdraws all non-local routes. End-host traffic destined for Campus 2/backbone is routed to mswitch0, which then generates ICMP Destination Net Unreachable (observed from 172.16.10.254) because it no longer has routes beyond Campus 1. Intra-VLAN and gateway reachability remain intact since mswitch0’s SVIs are up.

Localization evidence:
- mswitch0 OSPF up but no neighbors: “show ip ospf interface” on mswitch0 shows Neighbor Count 0 on br0; “show ip ospf neighbor” lists none.
- router16 FRR failed: systemctl shows frr “Active: failed (failed)”, and “show ip ospf neighbor” returns “ospfd is not running.”
- L2/L3 uplink is fine: mswitch0 can ping 10.2.2.1 successfully, indicating br0<->bond0 connectivity is up.
- Hosts see ICMP Net Unreachable from mswitch0 when pinging external subnets.

Root cause: The FRR/OSPF process on router16 is down, breaking the OSPF adjacency on 10.2.2.0/24 and removing external reachability from mswitch0.

Corrective fix: Restore routing on router16 by restarting FRR (e.g., systemctl restart frr) and verifying ospfd comes up and neighbors form on bond0 (10.2.2.1) with mswitch0 br0 (10.2.2.2). No changes required on mswitch0 or its uplink interfaces. 
=== END ===